H5  APPELL REFORMULATION:  Phi_n' = n Phi_{n-1}, so a common root of
    (I_m, I_{m+1}) is exactly a MULTIPLE root of Phi_{m+1}.
    s=1: Phi_n'=n Phi_(n-1) and Phi_n squarefree for n=1..8, and squarefreeness  <=>  gcd(I_m,I_(m+1))=1 : True
    s=2: Phi_n'=n Phi_(n-1) and Phi_n squarefree for n=1..8, and squarefreeness  <=>  gcd(I_m,I_(m+1))=1 : True
    s=3: Phi_n'=n Phi_(n-1) and Phi_n squarefree for n=1..8, and squarefreeness  <=>  gcd(I_m,I_(m+1))=1 : True
    s=4: Phi_n'=n Phi_(n-1) and Phi_n squarefree for n=1..8, and squarefreeness  <=>  gcd(I_m,I_(m+1))=1 : True

    Structural reason s=1 is easy and s>=2 is hard:
      generating function  sum Phi_n(z) xi^n/n! = e^{xi z} N(xi),  N(xi)=int e^{-xi t^s - t}dt.
      s=1: N(xi)=1/(1+xi), MEROMORPHIC (radius 1).  s>=2: N has Taylor coefficients
      (-1)^n (sn)!/n!, radius ZERO -- a Gevrey-s divergent series, no entire-function
      (Laguerre-Polya / Hermite-Biehler) machinery is available.

